      "ac": "C-02-AC3",
      "capability": "C-02",
      "capability_name": "Persistent memory and knowledge stores",
      "suite": "B4",
      "suite_kind": "family",
      "task_types": [
        "deletion propagation"
      ],
      "status": "slice_built",
      "existing_fragment": false,
      "blocked_by": [],
      "note": "B4 deletion and restore drills. M5 built the deletion slice (M5-B4-slice-2), extended it with backup/restore scenarios, then with interrupted partial restores and same-day retries, then moved the interruption point to the evaluator and added damaged-backup scenarios, and then added a record-write state model so an attempt can be interrupted inside a record rather than only between records; see docs/product/coverage-matrix.md section 9. No capability is validated. M5 then added a durability slice (M5-B4-slice-3, eval/tasks/b4w) that puts the same four-state audit on a synthetic write-ahead log and crashes it at evaluator-chosen byte offsets. It is test infrastructure, not a product store, and it changes no status. No capability is validated.",
      "slice": {
        "suite": "eval/tasks/b4d",
        "tasks": 27,
        "covers": "delete one or more records from index, cache and summary; post-delete probes; three deletion drills and a seven-day replay with zero resurrections; a backup taken before the deletion is restored during the replay, dropping deleted records and keeping unrelated ones; a restore interrupted after part of the backup and retried on the same day, where the retry duplicates no entry, resurrects no deleted record and leaves the final state recovered; a stop point the evaluator chooses from a candidate set the fixture only bounds, placed between records and also inside a record write, where each record carries not started / partially applied / committed / rolled back and the two legal recoveries (resume the parts already there, or roll the record back and rewrite it) must reach the same final state; three damaged-backup classes detected from a declared digest, two of which must be refused before any write and one repaired from the live store; sixteen survivors lost before the restore that only a complete restore can bring back",
        "sample_counts": {
          "tasks": 27,
          "drill_executions": 81,
          "replay_day_evaluations": 189,
          "backups": 19,
          "restore_events": 19,
          "restore_attempts": 31,
          "injected_stops": 5,
          "mid_write_stops": 5,
          "corrupt_backups": 3,
          "recovery_probes": 58,
          "probes": 87,
          "note": "deterministic executions, not independent samples; the scripted interruptions, partial writes, retries and damaged backups are scenarios, not a distribution over restore implementations"
        },
        "not_covered": "people-database field policy (Q18), real retention/legal-hold and replication semantics, corruption classes beyond the three synthetic ones, real checksum or key management, crash semantics in a real engine (no torn write, no fsync, no journal or WAL), a real store or real data"
      },
      "slice_2": {
        "suite": "eval/tasks/b4w",
        "slice_id": "M5-B4-slice-3",
        "tasks": 10,
        "covers": "a synthetic file-backed store with a write-ahead log, crashed by the evaluator at deterministic byte offsets during the log append, the record write, the commit marker and recovery replay: torn and corrupt tails, duplicate replay, a missing commit marker, a repeated crash during recovery, and one fail-closed fixture where damage sits before verifiable frames",
        "sample_counts": {
          "tasks": 10,
          "operations": 38,
          "wal_frames": 76,
          "planted_frames": 40,
          "crash_scenarios": 6,
          "simulated_crashes": 7,
          "bytes_truncated": 603,
          "declared_recovery_scenarios": 7,
          "duplicate_retry_operations": 1,
          "fail_closed_scenarios": 1,
          "note": "deterministic executions, not independent samples: each crash lands at one fixed byte, every scenario runs once, and no confidence interval is implied or may be computed from these counts"
        },
        "not_covered": "any real storage engine or real crash: there is no kernel, no disk cache, no power loss, no firmware reordering and no fsync semantics beyond flushing the file object the harness opens - the process dies because the harness raises at a byte offset; no real checksum or key management, no encryption at rest, no replication or consensus; nothing about performance (write amplification, fsync cost, recovery time), which is not measured; nothing about the J.A.R.V.I.S. product store - this is evaluator test infrastructure holding invented records; and no statistical claim: ten tasks, one deterministic execution each",
        "status": "slice_built"
      }
    },
